---
# IS ANY JVM ABOUT TO RUN OUT OF HEAP.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE FAILURE THIS EXISTS FOR
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# web-us OOMed on 2026-09-03T14:53 with `-Xmx768m` while a commit raising it to 1024m had been
# merged, green and tagged `deployed-web` for nearly three hours. NOTHING WAS RED. The CI deploy
# endpoint can only run `kubectl set image` (infra/nix/modules/roles/k8s-deploy.nix -- it "cannot
# run kubectl, and cannot name a resource"), so the new IMAGE rolled and the ConfigMap carrying
# JAVA_OPTS never moved. The tier ran for hours at 88% of a heap nobody believed it still had, and
# the first sign was a 502.
#
# So the rule below is deliberately NOT "did the config apply". Config drift is one way to arrive
# here; a corpus that outgrew its heap is another, and `web-uk` and `web-pl` are on that path today
# at 94.5% and 87.5% of their own caps. What both have in common is the only thing worth alerting
# on: A JVM WHOSE LIVE SET NO LONGER LEAVES ROOM TO COLLECT.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY OLD GEN, AND WHY NOT THE HEAP PEAK
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Peak heap ~= -Xmx is NORMAL -- that is when a collection runs, and every healthy pod here reads
# 93-99%. Alerting on it pages for health. The live set is the signal, and its proxy is the OLD
# GENERATION: tenured data that survived collection.
#
# `min_over_time(...[30m])` rather than the instantaneous value, because old-gen occupancy
# sawtooths -- it climbs with tenured garbage and drops when a mixed collection reclaims it. The
# floor of that sawtooth is what cannot be collected away, and it is the number that decides
# whether the next large allocation has anywhere to go. On web-us the floor walked 630 -> 725 MiB
# in the two hours before the OOM while the instantaneous value looked merely busy.
#
# ⚠️ THE FLOOR IS NOT THE LIVE SET, and this rule does not claim it is. G1 only truly compacts at a
# full collection, so the floor drifts upward under a stable live set too -- measured on this very
# incident, two forced full GCs 13 minutes apart showed the live set FLAT while the floor climbed.
# That is not a defect in the alert: a floor that has climbed past the threshold means the room
# left for allocation is gone whether the cause is retention or deferred compaction, and both end
# in the same OOM.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE POOL NAMES DIFFER BY COLLECTOR, WHICH IS WHY THE MATCH IS A REGEX
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# web runs G1 (`-XX:+UseG1GC`) and its tenured pool is `G1 Old Gen`; the workers run SerialGC and
# theirs is `Tenured Gen`. A rule naming only one silently covers half the fleet -- the exact shape
# k3s.rules warns about, where a series that never exists reads as coverage.
#
# ⚠️ HISTORY STARTS AT THE 2026-08-29 CUTOVER. `for:` windows longer than that were meaningless
# before it and are fine now, but do not reach further back when tuning these.
groups:
  - name: jvm-heap
    rules:
      - alert: JvmOldGenNearCap
        # The floor of the sawtooth, as a fraction of the pool's own cap.
        expr: >-
          min_over_time(jvm_memory_pool_used_bytes{pool=~"G1 Old Gen|Tenured Gen"}[30m])
            / on (instance, pool) group_left
          jvm_memory_pool_max_bytes{pool=~"G1 Old Gen|Tenured Gen"} > 0.85
        # TWO HOURS, because this is a trend and not an event. web-us took ~2h from restart to OOM,
        # so this fires with time to act on the pod that is going to die -- and a deploy rolling a
        # pod resets the measurement, which is what keeps a routine restart from paging.
        for: 2h
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.job }} {{ $labels.country }} old gen is {{ $value | humanizePercentage }} of its cap"
          description: >-
            The tenured floor has sat above 85% of this JVM's heap cap for two hours, so what is
            left is not enough to collect into and the next large allocation is the one that
            fails. This is the shape that OOM-killed web-us on 2026-09-03.
            FIRST CHECK THAT THE HEAP IS THE SIZE THE REPOSITORY THINKS IT IS -- Flux reconciles the ConfigMap now and Reloader rolls the pod on a real change, so a
            disagreement here means Flux is FAILING rather than merely waiting -- see flux.rules. Compare
            `kubectl -n kinowo get cm <tier>-env-<country> -o jsonpath='{.data.JAVA_OPTS}'`
            against the overlay. If they already agree, the corpus has outgrown the heap and the
            fix is the heap or the retention, not the ConfigMap.
